<div class="wrap">

  <!-- CASE HEADER -->
  <header class="case-header">
    <div class="case-meta">
      <span>Case No. 25-02</span>
      <span class="dot"></span>
      <span>Retail / E-Commerce</span>
      <span class="dot"></span>
      <span>MySQL · Tableau</span>
    </div>
    <h1 class="case-title">Superstore Sales<br>Analysis</h1>
    <p class="case-sub">Five SQL queries against 9,800 retail orders to identify where revenue comes from — by region, product, category, segment, and year — then visualized in a Tableau dashboard.</p>
    <div class="tag-row">
      <span class="tag filled">MySQL</span>
      <span class="tag filled">Tableau</span>
      <span class="tag">GROUP BY</span>
      <span class="tag">Aggregation</span>
      <span class="tag">Dashboard Design</span>
      <span class="tag">Business Intelligence</span>
    </div>
    <div class="header-cta">
      <a href="https://public.tableau.com/app/profile/braeden.berg/viz/SuperstoreSalesAnalysis_17863996966070/SuperstoreSalesAnalysis" target="_blank" class="btn btn-primary">View dashboard ↗</a>
      <a href="https://github.com/braedenberg13" target="_blank" class="btn btn-ghost">GitHub</a>
    </div>
  </header>

  <!-- STATS -->
  <div class="stats-row">
    <div class="stat">
      <div class="stat-n">9,800</div>
      <div class="stat-l">Rows analyzed</div>
    </div>
    <div class="stat">
      <div class="stat-n">$2.26M</div>
      <div class="stat-l">Total revenue</div>
    </div>
    <div class="stat">
      <div class="stat-n">5</div>
      <div class="stat-l">SQL queries</div>
    </div>
    <div class="stat">
      <div class="stat-n">4 yrs</div>
      <div class="stat-l">Data coverage</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- OBJECTIVE -->
  <section>
    <p class="section-eyebrow">01 — Objective</p>
    <h2 class="section-h">What I set out to answer</h2>
    <p class="section-p">Simulating the kind of analysis a data analyst would run for a sales or operations team — using raw order data to surface patterns that aren't obvious from a spreadsheet.</p>
    <ul class="obj-list">
      <li>Which region drives the most revenue, and by how much?</li>
      <li>What are the top 10 products by total sales?</li>
      <li>Which product categories and sub-categories perform best?</li>
      <li>How does revenue split across Consumer, Corporate, and Home Office segments?</li>
      <li>How has total revenue and order volume trended year over year?</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <!-- PROCESS -->
  <section>
    <p class="section-eyebrow">02 — Process</p>
    <h2 class="section-h">Step-by-step</h2>
    <p class="section-p">Each query was written from scratch in MySQL Workbench against a locally hosted database. Click any step to expand it.</p>

    <div class="steps">

      <!-- STEP 1 -->
      <div class="step">
        <div class="step-header" onclick="toggle(this)">
          <span class="step-n">Step 01</span>
          <span class="step-title-text">Data import & setup</span>
          <span class="step-chevron">▼</span>
        </div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <p class="step-desc-text">Created a dedicated MySQL database, defined a typed table schema matching the CSV columns, then loaded 9,800 rows using LOAD DATA INFILE.</p>
          <div class="code-block">
            <div class="code-top">
              <span class="code-label">SQL — Setup</span>
              <div class="code-dots"><span></span><span></span><span></span></div>
            </div>
            <pre><span class="kw">CREATE DATABASE</span> superstore;
<span class="kw">USE</span> superstore;

<span class="kw">CREATE TABLE</span> orders (
  row_id        <span class="fn">INT</span>,
  order_id      <span class="fn">VARCHAR</span>(<span class="str">20</span>),
  order_date    <span class="fn">VARCHAR</span>(<span class="str">20</span>),
  ship_date     <span class="fn">VARCHAR</span>(<span class="str">20</span>),
  ship_mode     <span class="fn">VARCHAR</span>(<span class="str">50</span>),
  customer_name <span class="fn">VARCHAR</span>(<span class="str">100</span>),
  segment       <span class="fn">VARCHAR</span>(<span class="str">50</span>),
  region        <span class="fn">VARCHAR</span>(<span class="str">20</span>),
  category      <span class="fn">VARCHAR</span>(<span class="str">50</span>),
  sub_category  <span class="fn">VARCHAR</span>(<span class="str">50</span>),
  product_name  <span class="fn">VARCHAR</span>(<span class="str">255</span>),
  sales         <span class="fn">DECIMAL</span>(<span class="str">10,4</span>)
);

<span class="kw">LOAD DATA INFILE</span> <span class="str">'C:/ProgramData/MySQL/.../superstore.csv'</span>
<span class="kw">INTO TABLE</span> orders
<span class="kw">FIELDS TERMINATED BY</span> <span class="str">','</span>
<span class="kw">ENCLOSED BY</span> <span class="str">'"'</span>
<span class="kw">LINES TERMINATED BY</span> <span class="str">'\n'</span>
<span class="kw">IGNORE</span> <span class="str">1</span> <span class="kw">ROWS</span>;</pre>
          </div>
          <div class="finding-box">
            <div class="finding-label">Result</div>
            <div class="finding-text">9,800 rows imported successfully across 18 columns. Zero errors, zero skipped rows.</div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- STEP 2 -->
      <div class="step">
        <div class="step-header" onclick="toggle(this)">
          <span class="step-n">Step 02</span>
          <span class="step-title-text">Query 1 — Sales by region</span>
          <span class="step-chevron">▼</span>
        </div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <p class="step-desc-text">Grouped total revenue by region using SUM() and GROUP BY to understand geographic performance distribution.</p>
          <div class="code-block">
            <div class="code-top">
              <span class="code-label">SQL — Query 1</span>
              <div class="code-dots"><span></span><span></span><span></span></div>
            </div>
            <pre><span class="kw">SELECT</span> region, <span class="fn">SUM</span>(sales) <span class="kw">AS</span> total_sales
<span class="kw">FROM</span> orders
<span class="kw">GROUP BY</span> region
<span class="kw">ORDER BY</span> total_sales <span class="op">DESC</span>;</pre>
          </div>
          <div class="finding-box">
            <div class="finding-label">Finding</div>
            <div class="finding-text">West led with $710K — 82% more than the South's $389K. The two eastern regions clustered closely in the middle at $492K and $669K.</div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- STEP 3 -->
      <div class="step">
        <div class="step-header" onclick="toggle(this)">
          <span class="step-n">Step 03</span>
          <span class="step-title-text">Query 2 — Top 10 products by sales</span>
          <span class="step-chevron">▼</span>
        </div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <p class="step-desc-text">Identified the highest revenue-generating individual products using GROUP BY on product name with a LIMIT of 10.</p>
          <div class="code-block">
            <div class="code-top">
              <span class="code-label">SQL — Query 2</span>
              <div class="code-dots"><span></span><span></span><span></span></div>
            </div>
            <pre><span class="kw">SELECT</span> product_name, <span class="fn">SUM</span>(sales) <span class="kw">AS</span> total_sales
<span class="kw">FROM</span> orders
<span class="kw">GROUP BY</span> product_name
<span class="kw">ORDER BY</span> total_sales <span class="op">DESC</span>
<span class="kw">LIMIT</span> <span class="str">10</span>;</pre>
          </div>
          <div class="finding-box">
            <div class="finding-label">Finding</div>
            <div class="finding-text">The Canon imageCLASS 2200 Copier generated $61,600 — more than double the second-place product at $27,453. The top 10 products alone accounted for a disproportionate share of total revenue.</div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- STEP 4 -->
      <div class="step">
        <div class="step-header" onclick="toggle(this)">
          <span class="step-n">Step 04</span>
          <span class="step-title-text">Query 3 — Sales by category & sub-category</span>
          <span class="step-chevron">▼</span>
        </div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <p class="step-desc-text">Used a multi-column GROUP BY to break down revenue at the sub-category level within each broader product category.</p>
          <div class="code-block">
            <div class="code-top">
              <span class="code-label">SQL — Query 3</span>
              <div class="code-dots"><span></span><span></span><span></span></div>
            </div>
            <pre><span class="kw">SELECT</span> category, sub_category,
       <span class="fn">SUM</span>(sales) <span class="kw">AS</span> total_sales
<span class="kw">FROM</span> orders
<span class="kw">GROUP BY</span> category, sub_category
<span class="kw">ORDER BY</span> total_sales <span class="op">DESC</span>;</pre>
          </div>
          <div class="finding-box">
            <div class="finding-label">Finding</div>
            <div class="finding-text">Technology Phones ($327K) and Furniture Chairs ($322K) were nearly tied at the top. Office Supplies Fasteners generated only $3K — the lowest of 17 sub-categories — suggesting a candidate for de-prioritization.</div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- STEP 5 -->
      <div class="step">
        <div class="step-header" onclick="toggle(this)">
          <span class="step-n">Step 05</span>
          <span class="step-title-text">Query 4 — Sales by customer segment</span>
          <span class="step-chevron">▼</span>
        </div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <p class="step-desc-text">Analyzed how total revenue distributed across the three customer segments: Consumer, Corporate, and Home Office.</p>
          <div class="code-block">
            <div class="code-top">
              <span class="code-label">SQL — Query 4</span>
              <div class="code-dots"><span></span><span></span><span></span></div>
            </div>
            <pre><span class="kw">SELECT</span> segment, <span class="fn">SUM</span>(sales) <span class="kw">AS</span> total_sales
<span class="kw">FROM</span> orders
<span class="kw">GROUP BY</span> segment
<span class="kw">ORDER BY</span> total_sales <span class="op">DESC</span>;</pre>
          </div>
          <div class="finding-box">
            <div class="finding-label">Finding</div>
            <div class="finding-text">Consumer drove $1.1M — nearly double Corporate at $688K. Home Office was the smallest at $425K. Consumer is clearly the core revenue driver and where retention efforts should focus.</div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- STEP 6 -->
      <div class="step">
        <div class="step-header" onclick="toggle(this)">
          <span class="step-n">Step 06</span>
          <span class="step-title-text">Query 5 — Year over year sales trend</span>
          <span class="step-chevron">▼</span>
        </div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <p class="step-desc-text">Used SUBSTRING() to extract the year from a text-formatted date column, then grouped revenue and unique order count by year to surface the business growth trajectory.</p>
          <div class="code-block">
            <div class="code-top">
              <span class="code-label">SQL — Query 5</span>
              <div class="code-dots"><span></span><span></span><span></span></div>
            </div>
            <pre><span class="kw">SELECT</span>
  <span class="fn">SUBSTRING</span>(order_date, <span class="str">7</span>, <span class="str">4</span>) <span class="kw">AS</span> year,
  <span class="fn">SUM</span>(sales)               <span class="kw">AS</span> total_sales,
  <span class="fn">COUNT</span>(<span class="op">DISTINCT</span> order_id)  <span class="kw">AS</span> total_orders
<span class="kw">FROM</span> orders
<span class="kw">GROUP BY</span> year
<span class="kw">ORDER BY</span> year <span class="op">ASC</span>;</pre>
          </div>
          <div class="finding-box">
            <div class="finding-label">Finding</div>
            <div class="finding-text">Revenue grew from $459K in 2016 to $722K in 2018 — a 57% increase. Order volume grew from 1,019 to 1,661 over the same period, confirming the growth was driven by volume, not price.</div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- DASHBOARD -->
  <section>
